use std
into build-sitemap.amni

Imports os. A command-line tool run from the site root that writes sitemap.xml for the site at "https://amni-scient.com", or, given the argument "--check", writes nothing and only reports problems. Every lookup by text uses a dictionary keyed by the 64-bit FNV-1a hash of that text, never by the text itself, because the native backend cannot index dictionaries with text keys.

`run_lines(command, scratch)` runs the shell command through the os module's run_command with its standard output sent to the scratch file, reads that file, deletes it with delete_file, and returns its nonblank lines with trailing carriage returns removed.

`page_list()` returns the site's page paths relative to the site root, from running "git -c core.quotepath=off ls-files -co --exclude-standard -- '*.html'" through `run_lines` with scratch file "tools/.sitemap-pages". It drops a path when its file name is "404.html" or starts with an underscore, and when any folder in the path is named ".git", ".github", "node_modules", "assets", "tests", "tools", "scripts", "docs", "backups", "x", "_shared", "vendor" or "pkg", or starts with an underscore or a dot.

`robots_rules()` reads robots.txt and returns, for every line whose trimmed text starts with "disallow:" in any letter case, the trimmed text after its first colon, skipping the empty ones.

`disallowed(path, rules)` says whether a page path that starts with a slash is blocked by any of the rules. A rule matches when it matches the beginning of the path, where a star stands for any run of characters including none, a dollar sign as the last character means the path must end at that point, and every other character must match exactly.

`tag_attr(html, tag, key, value, want)` finds the first tag with the given name in the page whose attribute named key equals value, ignoring letter case in tag and attribute names and in the compared value, and returns the text of its attribute named want, or empty text when there is no such tag or attribute. Attributes may come in any order and their values may be wrapped in double or single quotes.

`served(address)` returns the address the host really serves for it: a trailing "index.html" is removed while the slash before it stays, otherwise a trailing ".html" is removed, and any other address is returned unchanged.

`commit_dates(today)` returns a dictionary from the 64-bit FNV-1a hash of a page path to the date of the newest commit that touched it, from running "git -c core.quotepath=off log --format=@%cs --name-only -- '*.html'" through `run_lines` with scratch file "tools/.sitemap-log": a line starting with "@" sets the current date to the text after it, and every other line is a path that takes the current date unless the map already has one. Then every path listed by "git -c core.quotepath=off status --porcelain --untracked-files=all -- '*.html'", run the same way with scratch file "tools/.sitemap-status", is set to today; its path is the text after the first three characters of the line, or the text after " -> " when the line has one.

`main()` gets today's date as the first line of running "date +%F" with scratch file "tools/.sitemap-today". For every page from `page_list` that robots.txt does not block (its path is a slash followed by the page path) and whose robots meta tag content does not contain "noindex" in any letter case, the page's address is its canonical link's href when that starts with "https://amni-scient.com", and otherwise "https://amni-scient.com/" followed by the page path; either way it then goes through `served`. Its date comes from `commit_dates`, or today when the page has none. When two pages share an address keep the later date, comparing dates as text, finding earlier entries through a dictionary keyed by the 64-bit FNV-1a hash of the address. The entries are sorted by address length, shortest first, then by the address bytes. The sitemap text is the line "<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>", then the line "<urlset xmlns=\"http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9\">", then one line "<url><loc>ADDRESS</loc><lastmod>DATE</lastmod></url>" per entry, then the line "</urlset>", every line ending in a newline. Without "--check" it writes that text to sitemap.xml and prints "sitemap: N urls". With "--check" it writes nothing; for each indexed page whose canonical href or og:url meta content ends in ".html" it prints "canonical points at a redirect: PATH VALUE"; it reads the current sitemap.xml, collects the text between every "<loc>" and "</loc>", prints, using a dictionary keyed by the 64-bit FNV-1a hash of each address, "sitemap is missing ADDRESS" for each new address it lacks and "sitemap has extra ADDRESS" for each of its addresses not among the new ones, never comparing dates. When it printed any problem it prints "rebuild with: hc run tools/build-sitemap.amni" and ends the process with status 1 through Helium's built-in exit, which flushes output itself; otherwise it prints "sitemap: ok, N urls".
